        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
