      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ffe3571c-83fb-57c7-a2fb-20b07ea00b69",
        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=x + 2$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} 3 e^{x + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x + 2$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=2\\\\x=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} 3 e^{x + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=3\\left(\\int_{2}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 2} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\int_{2}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 2} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
