    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186184352,
        "id": "114e3660-63d9-511d-b89c-e71081ffa1e5",
        "title": "Evaluating Definite Integrals"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "1493bcad-9473-5e45-a62f-7de7e087075a",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} 4 \\cdot 3^{2 x^{2} + 2} x\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int 3^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{3^{u}}{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}+C$.\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x^{2} + 2$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(4 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=2\\\\x=1&\\Longrightarrow u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{1} 4 \\cdot 3^{2 x^{2} + 2} x\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_{2}^{4} 3^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the supplied primitive and subtract lower from upper:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\frac{3^{u}}{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}\\right]_{2}^{4}=\\left(\\frac{81}{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{9}{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe exact answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "d9f78162-d59e-5a3c-b279-66ccfe2509f4",
            ":answer-field/key": "answer",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
            ":answer-field/choices": [
              {
                ":answer/id": "1d2cc3bd-449b-51d2-9b3f-7058a9173358",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "\\frac{72}{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}"
              }
            ],
            ":answer-field/correct": [
              ":answer/id",
              "1d2cc3bd-449b-51d2-9b3f-7058a9173358"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
